---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import toolsData from '../data/tools.json';

const { tools, priceVerified } = toolsData;

// Cheapest month-to-month entry price first. That is the number people compare,
// and it is what you actually pay if you just sign up today without committing
// to a year.
const rows = [...tools].sort((a, b) => a.priceFrom - b.priceFrom);

const cheapestPaid = rows[0];
const lovable = tools.find(t => t.id === 'lovable');
const withAnnual = rows.filter(t => t.priceAnnual !== null);

// Render the verification date in a fixed locale so the static build does not
// depend on the build machine's regional settings.
const verifiedLabel = new Date(priceVerified).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric',
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AI App Builder Pricing Tracker"
  description="What every major AI app builder actually costs in 2026: month-to-month price, annual price, and free tier. Verified against vendor pricing pages, with the date we checked."
>
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12">

    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-3" style="color: var(--color-text);">
      AI app builder pricing tracker
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg mb-4" style="color: var(--color-muted);">
      Entry prices for every builder reviewed on this site, side by side, with the
      month-to-month and annual numbers separated - because the difference between
      them is where most published comparisons quietly go wrong.
    </p>

    <p class="text-sm mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2" style="color: var(--color-muted);">
      <span
        class="inline-block w-2 h-2 rounded-full"
        style="background-color: var(--color-success);"
        aria-hidden="true"
      ></span>
      <strong style="color: var(--color-text);">Last verified: {verifiedLabel}</strong>
      <span>· every price re-checked against the vendor's own pricing page plus two independent sources.</span>
    </p>

    <div class="disclosure-notice">
      <span class="font-bold shrink-0 mt-0.5" style="color: var(--color-accent);">ℹ</span>
      <span>
        Some links here are affiliate links. They never change the order of this
        table, which is sorted by price.&#32;
        <a href="/disclosure" class="hover:underline ml-1" style="color: var(--color-accent-light);">Full disclosure →</a>
      </span>
    </div>

    <!-- Comparison table -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl" style="border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
      <table class="w-full text-sm" style="border-collapse: collapse;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="background-color: var(--color-surface-2);">
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3" style="color: var(--color-text);">Tool</th>
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap" style="color: var(--color-text);">Month&#8209;to&#8209;month</th>
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap" style="color: var(--color-text);">Billed yearly</th>
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap" style="color: var(--color-text);">Free tier</th>
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3" style="color: var(--color-text);">Worth paying for if</th>
            {/* relative: sr-only is absolutely positioned, and without a
                positioned ancestor it escapes the scroll container and widens
                the whole document on mobile. */}
            <th class="px-4 py-3 relative"><span class="sr-only">Links</span></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map(tool => (
            <tr style="border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top">
                <div class="font-semibold" style="color: var(--color-text);">{tool.name}</div>
                <a href={`/blog/${tool.reviewSlug}`} class="link-accent text-xs">Read the review</a>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top whitespace-nowrap" style="color: var(--color-prose-body);">
                ${tool.priceFrom}/mo
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top whitespace-nowrap" style="color: var(--color-prose-body);">
                {tool.priceAnnual === null
                  ? <span style="color: var(--color-muted);">No annual rate</span>
                  : <>${tool.priceAnnual}/mo</>}
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top" style="color: var(--color-prose-body);">
                {tool.freeTier ? 'Yes' : 'No'}
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top" style="color: var(--color-prose-body);">
                {tool.bestFor}
                <div class="text-xs mt-1" style="color: var(--color-muted);">Skip it for: {tool.notFor}</div>
                <div class="text-xs mt-1" style="color: var(--color-muted);">{tool.priceNote}</div>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top">
                <a
                  href={`/go/${tool.id}`}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="nofollow sponsored noopener"
                  data-tool={tool.id}
                  class="btn-primary py-1.5 px-3 text-xs whitespace-nowrap"
                >
                  Try it →
                </a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Guidance -->
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none mt-12">
      <h2>How this table is put together</h2>

      <p>
        Two numbers, not one. Nearly every vendor advertises the annual price as
        if it were the monthly price, so a comparison that lists a single figure
        per tool is usually comparing one company's discounted yearly rate
        against another's full month-to-month rate. {withAnnual.length} of these
        {' '}{rows.length} tools charge less if you commit to a year; the rest
        publish one rate or no reliable annual figure at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        The entry price is still the least useful number here. Every one of these
        tools bills against something that runs out - messages, credits, workload
        units, compute minutes - and the tier you actually end up on depends on
        how much you build rather than which plan you picked on day one. That is
        what the note under each row is for.
      </p>

      <p>
        All {rows.length} let you start without paying. Use that. Build the same
        small thing on two of them and you will learn more in an afternoon than
        any comparison table can tell you, including this one.
      </p>

      <h2>If you're picking one to pay for</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>You can already code.</strong> {cheapestPaid.name} is the cheapest
        paid option in the table at ${cheapestPaid.priceFrom}/mo, and it's the right
        first step if you mostly want faster autocomplete rather than an agent
        rewriting files. Cursor costs twice that and earns it once you're doing
        multi-file work.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>You can't, and you want a real app.</strong> Lovable, at
        ${lovable.priceFrom}/mo month-to-month or ${lovable.priceAnnual}/mo on a
        year. It costs more than an editor because it hands you a database and
        auth without you understanding either. That's the thing you're actually
        buying, and it's also the thing that will bite you the first time
        something breaks. The free tier is five credits a day, which is enough to
        find out whether you like it and not enough to ship anything.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>You want a site, not an app.</strong> Framer or Webflow. Framer
        gets you there faster and cheaper, Webflow gives you more room to grow.
        Watch the billing cycle on both: each roughly doubles in price if you pay
        monthly instead of yearly, and Webflow's site plan is separate from the
        workspace seat you may also end up paying for. Neither one needs the AI
        builders at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>You're not sure yet.</strong> Don't pay anyone. Start on Bolt's
        free tier, get something ugly working, and let that tell you which of the
        problems above you actually have.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>

      <p>
        Prices move and vendors rename plans without notice. If something here is
        out of date, tell me and I'll fix it and re-date the table:
        {' '}<a href="mailto:hello@aibuilderhub.app">hello@aibuilderhub.app</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>
